  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Serbian Climate Resilience &amp;amp;amp; Irrigation Programme SCRIP</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Provision of a sovereign loan to the Republic of Serbia (the &quot;Client&quot;) in an  amount of EUR 48 million to finance the construction and rehabilitation of  critical irrigation infrastructure in three distinct regions of Serbia: Negotin  (Eastern Serbia), Svilajnac (Central Serbia) and Vojvodina (Northern Serbia).  The investments will be financed from an initial loan to the Republic of Serbia  in the amount of up to EUR 30 million and Loan Extensions totalling EUR 18  million (to be signed in 2023). Financing of the Programme is split into two  phases: (i) Loan I (for investments in Negotin and Svilajnac) of up to EUR 15  million, which was signed in December 2019 and which will be completed by a  Loan Extension of EUR 10 million, and (ii) Loan II (for investments in  Vojvodina) of up to EUR 15 million, which was signed in 2021 and which will be  completed by a Loan Extension of EUR 8 million.  The Programme is being implemented by two Public Water Management Companies  (&quot;PMWC&quot;), PMWC Srbijavode for Loan I and PMWC Vode Vojvodine for Loan II, in  coordination with the Ministry of Agriculture, Forestry, and Water Management  (&quot;MAFWM&quot;), and respective municipalities.  The Loan Extensions are required to cover (i) recent material costs&apos; increase  that have negatively impacted the total investment cost, and (ii) project  preparation for the Pambukovica dam, a critical infrastructure investment to  ensure flooding protection and which, at later stage, will serve irrigation  purposes.  The Programme also entails continuation of policy dialogue with the MAFWM by  assisting in preparation of the country&apos;s first Irrigation Strategy and a  five-year Action Plan.  Agriculture plays an important economic role in Serbia, representing 6 per cent  of the GDP. However, irrigation infrastructure is obsolete and irrigated areas  remain constrained through a lack of sustainable water resources.  Moreover, the country&apos;s climate related vulnerability is increasing  (according to recent estimates, 30 per cent of the total country area and 52  per cent of agricultural land is vulnerable to rainwater related flooding or  water logging).  Therefore, the Government of Serbia has decided to engage in upgrading,  expanding and modernising its irrigation infrastructure to sustain Serbia&apos;s  climate resilience and support its agricultural sector, including a shift  towards higher yield-crops. Additionally, the rehabilitation of Serbia&apos;s aged  irrigation and water infrastructure is expected to contribute to water and  energy savings.</narrative>
  </description>
